# Lobster Browser — cloud backend environment.
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values locally. NEVER commit `.env` (see root .gitignore).
# These are PLACEHOLDERS ONLY — do not put real secrets in this example file.

# HTTP port the API listens on (main.ts falls back to 8080 if unset).
PORT=8080
# The production service sits behind nginx and `main.ts` trusts one proxy hop. Binding loopback keeps
# clients from reaching Node directly and forging X-Forwarded-For to evade per-IP rate limits.
HOST=127.0.0.1

# --- Rate limits ---
# In-process, per route class (apps/backend/src/rate-limit/rate-limit.ts). A budget is spent by the
# verified principal where there is one — the team for /agent/llm/*, the signed-in user elsewhere —
# and by the address only for anonymous traffic, so an office behind one NAT never shares a budget.
# Defaults per minute: auth 30 (always per address: it is the credential-guessing surface), sync 240,
# leases 300, agent 600, general 300. nginx's edge zones (deploy/nginx/lobster-limits.conf) still
# answer first on the auth and billing routes. RATE_LIMIT_MAX, the old single knob, sets `general`.
#RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
#RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=30
#RATE_LIMIT_SYNC_MAX=240
#RATE_LIMIT_LEASES_MAX=300
#RATE_LIMIT_AGENT_MAX=600
#RATE_LIMIT_GENERAL_MAX=300

# --- Database (Postgres, consumed by Prisma `datasource`) ---
# Format: postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE?schema=public
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://lobster:lobster@localhost:5432/lobster?schema=public

# --- Auth ---
# Symmetric secret used to sign/verify JWTs. REQUIRED in production — the server refuses to start
# with the public dev secret when NODE_ENV=production. In dev/test it may be omitted (a dev secret
# is used). Leave DATABASE_URL unset locally to use the in-memory user store (no Postgres needed).
JWT_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string

# --- Profile vault ---
# 32 random bytes, base64, wrapping the per-account key that opens every profile snapshot. Kept out
# of the database on purpose: with it, a stolen dump, backup or replica is inert; without it, a copy
# of Postgres plus the blob store is every user's cookies and live sessions in the clear.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# REQUIRED in production. Losing it makes every stored profile snapshot unreadable, so back it up
# wherever JWT_SECRET is backed up. Set ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_VAULT_KEYS=1 to run without one anyway.
VAULT_MASTER_KEY=replace-with-32-random-bytes-base64
#ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_VAULT_KEYS=1

# --- Managed Lobee agent (OpenRouter) ---
#
# THE ONE OPERATOR CREDENTIAL. Lobee runs in "managed" mode: this backend holds a single OpenRouter
# key and no client ever sees it. The desktop mints a short-lived agent JWT, the sidecar sends that
# as its bearer to `/agent/llm/*`, and the proxy swaps it for this key on the way to OpenRouter.
#
# WITHOUT IT the API still starts and everything else keeps working, but the agent is dead:
# `/agent/llm/chat/completions` answers 503, `/agent/llm/models` serves an empty roster, and the
# panel shows "Lobee is temporarily unavailable". Boot is deliberately NOT failed over one module —
# instead the backend logs a warning naming this variable at startup and reports
# `credential: "missing"` on `GET /health/agent`, so the gap is visible before a customer finds it.
#
# THE BALANCE BEHIND IT IS THE OPERATOR'S, NOT THE CUSTOMER'S. An empty one is an outage: every
# managed run fails and the journal carries `agent/llm OPERATOR_FAULT`. Nobody is billed for it and
# nobody is asked to top anything up, so nothing but that log line will tell you. Watch it.
# Issue one at https://openrouter.ai/keys.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-replace-me

# Optional agent tuning. Every value is clamped, so a typo degrades rather than breaks.
#
#   AGENT_ALLOWED_MODELS         Comma-separated OpenRouter model ids allowed IN ADDITION to the
#                                built-in brands (openai/*, anthropic/*, google/*). Entries that are
#                                not a valid `vendor/model` id, or longer than 300 characters, are
#                                dropped. Default: empty, i.e. those three brands and nothing else.
#   AGENT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS      Hard ceiling on `max_tokens` for every forwarded completion, and the
#                                figure the pre-flight Credit reserve is priced against. A client may
#                                ask for less, never for more. Default 8192, capped at 32768.
#   AGENT_MODEL_SYNC_TIMEOUT_MS  How long the (hourly, cached) OpenRouter catalog sync may take
#                                before the roster falls back to the last good cache — or to an empty
#                                one on a cold start. Default 10000, capped at 120000.
#   AGENT_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS    Deadline on one chat completion. The sidecar does not retry a
#                                managed timeout, so this is the longest one agent step can hang.
#                                Default 55000, capped at 120000.
#AGENT_ALLOWED_MODELS=
#AGENT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192
#AGENT_MODEL_SYNC_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
#AGENT_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS=55000

# --- Billing (NOWPayments) ---
#
# Payment is CRYPTO ONLY. Users hold a USD "Credit" balance topped up by on-chain deposits, and a
# package purchase or monthly renewal debits it. There is no card processor and no Stripe.
#
# Both values come from the NOWPayments dashboard. With either one unset, deposit creation returns
# 503 and IPN callbacks are rejected — the app still runs, but no Credit can be added.

# Dashboard -> Settings -> Payments -> API keys.
NOWPAYMENTS_API_KEY=replace-me

# Dashboard -> Settings -> Payments -> IPN secret key. This verifies that a callback really came
# from NOWPayments, and it is the ONLY thing standing between a POST to /billing/webhook and
# minted Credit. Treat it exactly as seriously as the API key.
NOWPAYMENTS_IPN_SECRET=replace-me

# Public HTTPS URL NOWPayments should call when a payment changes state. Must be reachable from
# the internet — a localhost value silently means deposits are never credited.
NOWPAYMENTS_IPN_CALLBACK_URL=https://api.lobrowser.com/billing/webhook

# Optional. Both default OFF and are only sent when set to "true".
#
#   FEE_PAID_BY_USER  charges the processor's commission to the customer instead of to us.
#                     Sent as `is_fee_paid_by_user`.
#   FIXED_RATE        locks the quoted crypto/USD rate for the payment window, so a price move
#                     between quote and settlement cannot leave the deposit short. NOWPayments
#                     charges a higher commission for it. Sent as `is_fixed_rate`.
#NOWPAYMENTS_FEE_PAID_BY_USER=false
#NOWPAYMENTS_FIXED_RATE=false

# --- Subscription renewal ---
# How often to sweep for subscriptions whose period has ended and charge Credit again. Periods are
# calendar months (or twelve of them, for a package paid a year up front) anchored to the day the
# team bought on. The sweep is safe to run concurrently on every instance (the charge is a
# compare-and-swap), so no external scheduler is needed. Set to 0 to disable it and drive renewals
# externally instead — see BILLING_ADMIN_TOKEN, without which 0 means nothing renews at all.
#RENEWAL_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# Shared secret for the operator-only billing endpoints, sent as the `X-Admin-Token` header:
#
#   curl -fsS -X POST -H "X-Admin-Token: $TOKEN" https://api.lobrowser.com/billing/admin/renewal-sweep
#
# This is how a deployment with RENEWAL_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=0 charges anything at all. Unset means
# those endpoints refuse every caller, which is the safe default but also means an external cron has
# no way in. Generate: node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#BILLING_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# --- Deposit reconciliation ---
# How often to ask the processor about deposits that have not settled, instead of waiting to be
# told. This is the floor under the IPN: verifying a callback means reproducing NOWPayments' own
# JSON serialisation byte for byte, and if that ever diverges then EVERY callback is rejected at
# once and no deposit is credited. Pulling the state makes that outage a delay instead. Set to 0 to
# disable. Safe to run on every instance (crediting is idempotent on the payment id).
#DEPOSIT_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS=600000

# --- Teams ---
# How many teams one account may OWN, its personal team included. A team is a way to organise and
# share profiles, not a unit of allowance — the profile limit belongs to the account and every team
# it owns draws on the same one — so nothing legitimate needs many, and without a cap `POST /teams`
# is the cheapest way for anyone with a JWT to create rows here without bound. Never below 1; a
# value that is not a number means the default, not "unlimited".
#TEAMS_PER_ACCOUNT_LIMIT=3

# --- Housekeeping ---
# How often to drop rows that have outlived their purpose: abandoned sign-ups (which hold a
# password hash for an account that was never created), redeemed desktop authorisation grants,
# consumed verification codes and expired profile leases. Set to 0 to disable and sweep externally.
#HOUSEKEEPING_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# --- Encrypted profile blob storage (S3-compatible) ---
# Encrypted profile blobs live in object storage; Postgres only holds a reference + metadata.
# Setting S3_BUCKET selects the real S3BlobStore; leave it unset to use the in-memory store
# (local dev / tests). For MinIO in dev: S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000 (path-style addressing
# is enabled automatically whenever a custom endpoint is set; override with S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE).
# Omit the key pair to use the AWS SDK's default credential chain (IAM role, ~/.aws, env).
S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.amazonaws.com
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=lobster-profiles
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=replace-me
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=replace-me
# Optional key namespace inside the bucket, e.g. "profiles" -> profiles/<team>/<profile>/<v>.enc
#S3_KEY_PREFIX=
# Optional: "true"/"false" to force/disable path-style URLs (defaults to true with S3_ENDPOINT).
#S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=
# --- OR: encrypted blobs on this server's own disk ---
# A directory is a first-class choice, not a fallback: the client encrypts before upload, so the
# server stores opaque bytes and only needs durability and atomicity. Preferred over S3 for a
# single-server deployment — no external dependency, no credentials. Recent versions are retained
# (see BLOB_RETAIN_VERSIONS), so point-in-time recovery is possible; back it up alongside the database.
#BLOB_STORE_PATH=/var/lib/lobster/blobs
#
# --- Blob retention and quota (both stores) ---
# How many versions of each profile's snapshot to keep. After every push the older versions beyond
# this many are deleted; the newest is never a candidate (the floor is 1). The desktop pushes after
# every stop and every dirty reconcile tick, so without this the store grew by every snapshot ever
# taken. Five is enough for "give me yesterday's cookies".
#BLOB_RETAIN_VERSIONS=5
#
# Storage quota per team for LIVE bytes — the latest snapshot of every profile; retained history is
# bounded by BLOB_RETAIN_VERSIONS and does not count — on the free allowance, in bytes. A team whose
# account is entitled to more profiles gets proportionally more (a Plus account's 100 profiles get
# 100/3 of this). A push that would exceed it is refused with HTTP 507 and a message the launcher
# shows verbatim. 0 disables the check; a value that is not a number means the default.
#BLOB_TEAM_QUOTA_BYTES=262144000
#
# In production, having NEITHER S3_BUCKET nor BLOB_STORE_PATH refuses to boot rather than silently
# using the process-local in-memory store, which loses every pushed blob on restart while the API
# keeps handing clients version numbers that promise durability. Set this to acknowledge that anyway.
#ALLOW_EPHEMERAL_BLOB_STORE=1
